  "slug": "heat-stress-95b0",
  "summary": "New work links CCA1/LHY, DOF/REVEILLE, MYB/AP2-ERF and m6A regulators to timing and decoupling of stress and developmental programs.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is regulatory control over when and how plants deploy stress-related programs. In Arabidopsis, CCA1 and LHY mutants alter time-of-day heat responses across transcriptome, proteome and metabolome, implicating sulfur and ROS processes. In cultivated potato, diel expression phase differs from wild Solanum species independently of clock period, with DOF and REVEILLE factors linked to the shifts. In white spruce, autumn warming decouples photosynthesis and cold-acclimation networks from growth cessation, with MYB and AP2/ERF regulators implicated. Two Plant Physiology studies add m6A-based control: MeRIP-seq and metabolomics in drought-contrasting wheat, and the cold-repressed demethylase PpALKBH9 stabilizing PpFUL4 mRNA to promote ethylene-associated peach ripening. New emphasis falls on phase shifts, program decoupling and epitranscriptomic regulation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
